# Choreographed Checkout Saga - Design

## Service boundaries

| Service | Owns (data) | Publishes | Subscribes to |
|---|---|---|---|
| Orders | orders, saga state, outbox | OrderPlaced, OrderConfirmed, CompensationTriggered | PaymentSucceeded, PaymentFailed, InventoryReserved, InventoryFailed |
| Payments | payments, refunds | PaymentSucceeded, PaymentFailed | OrderPlaced, CompensationTriggered |
| Inventory | stock reservations | InventoryReserved, InventoryFailed | OrderPlaced, CompensationTriggered |
| Notifications | sent-notification ledger | NotificationSent | OrderConfirmed |

Each service owns its data exclusively. Services communicate only through
events on the broker - no shared database, no cross-service table access, and
no synchronous call chains.

## Event choreography

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant C as Client
    participant O as Orders
    participant B as Broker
    participant P as Payments
    participant I as Inventory
    participant N as Notifications

    C->>O: POST /checkout
    O->>O: persist order + outbox row (local tx)
    O->>B: OrderPlaced
    B->>P: OrderPlaced
    B->>I: OrderPlaced
    P->>P: capture payment
    P->>B: PaymentSucceeded
    I->>I: reserve stock
    I->>B: InventoryReserved
    B->>O: PaymentSucceeded + InventoryReserved
    O->>O: saga state -> CONFIRMED
    O->>B: OrderConfirmed
    B->>N: OrderConfirmed
    N-->>C: confirmation email
    Note over I: stock short
    I->>B: InventoryFailed
    B->>O: InventoryFailed
    O->>O: saga state -> COMPENSATING
    O->>B: CompensationTriggered
    B->>P: CompensationTriggered
    P->>P: refund payment
```

## Failure handling and compensation

If a participant fails, it publishes its failure event and the saga
compensates in reverse order of the steps that succeeded:

- Inventory short -> `InventoryFailed` -> Orders runs compensation ->
  `CompensationTriggered` -> Payments refunds (idempotent by payment id).
- Payment declined -> `PaymentFailed` -> no compensation needed (nothing
  committed after the order itself, which stays cancelled).

Compensating actions are business-level undos (refund the payment, release the
reservation), never database rollbacks. Each compensation is idempotent and
retried with backoff; if retries are exhausted the order goes to a manual
resolution queue. Every participant reacts to both success and failure events.

## The outbox pattern

Events are published reliably with a transactional outbox. In the SAME local
transaction that changes state, each service writes a row to its outbox table;
a relay (polling publisher or CDC such as Debezium) publishes outbox rows to
the broker, which delivers at-least-once. No event is lost between the database
write and the broker.

## Idempotent consumers

Because delivery is at-least-once, consumers deduplicate: they record the
processed event id in the same transaction as the effect (a unique
processed_events key), so redeliveries and retries are no-ops. Outgoing calls
use idempotency keys (e.g. refund by refund id) so retries are safe.
MD

cat > events.py <<'PY'
"""Domain events for the choreographed checkout saga.

Each event is an immutable dataclass carrying an order_id (the saga aggregate)
and an event_id (for idempotent consumers). Published by exactly one service
via its outbox; consumed by the services that subscribe to it.
"""

from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from uuid import UUID, uuid4


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class OrderPlaced:
    order_id: str
    customer_id: str
    total_cents: int
    event_id: UUID = field(default_factory=uuid4)


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class PaymentSucceeded:
    order_id: str
    payment_id: str
    event_id: UUID = field(default_factory=uuid4)


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class PaymentFailed:
    order_id: str
    reason: str
    event_id: UUID = field(default_factory=uuid4)


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class InventoryReserved:
    order_id: str
    reservation_id: str
    event_id: UUID = field(default_factory=uuid4)


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class InventoryFailed:
    order_id: str
    unavailable_skus: list
    event_id: UUID = field(default_factory=uuid4)


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class CompensationTriggered:
    order_id: str
    reason: str
    event_id: UUID = field(default_factory=uuid4)


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class OrderConfirmed:
    order_id: str
    event_id: UUID = field(default_factory=uuid4)


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class NotificationSent:
    order_id: str
    channel: str
    event_id: UUID = field(default_factory=uuid4)
PY

